    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Delhi Sultanate comprised five successive dynasties: Mamluk (Slave), Khilji, Tughlaq, Sayyid, and Lodi.",
        "Qutb-ud-din Aibak established the Sultanate in 1206 AD and commenced the construction of the Qutb Minar.",
        "Iltutmish consolidated the empire, introduced the Iqtadari system, organized Turkan-i-Chahalgani, and minted Silver Tanka and Copper Jital coins.",
        "Alauddin Khilji introduced revolutionary market price control reforms and military measures including Dagh (branding of horses) and Chehra (descriptive roll of soldiers).",
        "Muhammad bin Tughlaq implemented major administrative experiments such as token currency and shifting the capital to Daulatabad.",
        "The First Battle of Panipat in 1526 ended the Delhi Sultanate and paved the way for the Mughal Empire."
      ],
      "id": "f4ap0g6"
    },
